# Bounded whole-word and regular-expression saved-file search

Saved-file search provides boolean `wholeWord` and `useRegex` options, default false, with labelled UI checkboxes. Query and Match case keep their existing contract. Unknown input fields retain the preceding API's ignored-field behavior: they do not activate flags, settings, multiline, globs or other features. Existing saved UTF-8 filesystem/path/byte/output limits and exact byte revisions/UTF-16 positions remain. New option changes invalidate result publication and navigation. Draft and corrected Save request/content/revision/publication guards remain.

Whole-word construction follows the finite pinned helper contract: test first/last source characters for ASCII word edges, then add `\b` without grouping alternation. Regex `a|b` becomes `\ba|b\b`; literal `é` receives no added boundaries. New runtime code normalizes `\w`/`\W`/`\b`/`\B` to Unicode Alphabetic/Mark/Decimal_Number/Connector_Punctuation/join-control word characters, `\d`/`\D` to Unicode decimal digits, and `\s`/`\S` to White_Space. Negated shorthands inside bracket classes (`[\W]`, `[\D]`, `[\S]`) are explicitly unsupported. Applicable JavaScript Unicode syntax/lookaround/backreferences compile only in a worker; Match case controls case folding.

Matching is single-line: LF separates lines, trailing CR is excluded, no match crosses lines. Empty files and the virtual line after terminal LF are excluded. Empty matches advance one Unicode scalar and never split a surrogate pair. Released ripgrep empty-match iteration differs: retained probes contain UTF-8 interior-byte offsets and suppressed terminal/adjacent empty matches. Released `--crlf` anchors at bare CR differ too. JavaScript dot/anchors at U+2028/U+2029, property names, syntax and case folding need not match Rust/PCRE2. These are compatibility limits, not universal original equivalence.

One request owns one worker; at most four active workers are admitted across workspaces in this module instance. A fifth direct API request is immediately refused. The current HTTP host separately serializes Editor operations; the admission bound does not promise browser concurrency outside that queue. Worker bounds: query32KiB, content2MiB,1001 spans per match request, response2MiB; V8 old generation64MiB, young generation16MiB, stack4MiB. Five seconds includes startup and file matching. Timeout terminates and awaits worker exit, then returns previously completed-file rows with explicit incomplete `regex-time-limit`; worker failure uses `regex-worker-failed`. Failed-file partial rows are not published. Invalid/unsupported patterns fail before walking. Success/failure closes workers; exported `dispose(workspace)` rejects active matching and awaits owned exits.

No AbortSignal or remote UI cancel is provided. Options/view disposal invalidate client publication, while bounded server work may continue. These budgets are not a hard whole-process memory watchdog: message copies, parent buffers, V8 overhead, OS I/O, scheduling and termination latency lie outside individual heap limits; a blocked filesystem call can delay completion. Runtime uses Node standard-library modules only and imports no original executable/library. Only server code imports helpers/workers, so no browser helper route is required.

Verification-only authority is VS Code1.140.0 commit `07f806f999227108933c2e30515b26eecc1fda74`. Complete official MIT files and URL/bytes/SHA/line identities are retained at `official-source-provenance.json`. Selected strings.ts escape/createRegExp and ripgrepTextSearchEngine.ts unicodeEscapesToPCRE2 helpers are separately evaluated, not an original search service/extension host. Shipped ripgrep15.0.0 rev3a612f88b8 SHA256 is `ba5105e5c9d2beb2af7b64d53d782a780f1d53e2a5e331d9ac71b06348cb533c`. Original engine calls and helper calls are counted separately. Full workbench/extension host, Rust/PCRE2 language, multiline, glob/ignore settings, remote providers, unsaved-buffer search and replace-across-files remain open. No performance acceptance.

Earlier search140/124/126 and Save epochs remain distinct historical evidence. Author-selected prepared comparisons are finite, not independent held-out coverage. Initial assertion errors and original divergences remain preserved.

Exact upstream source/license files and failed/corrected study epochs are mapped by the [immutable search evidence manifest](../../results/editor-search-pattern-reviewed-installation-20261013/source-proof/preservation-manifest.json).
